1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,421
Hi everyone, welcome to the Snow Centre. My name's Annie.

2
00:00:05,421 --> 00:00:09,680
I hope you enjoyed the bus trip from the airport

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:13,100
we've certainly got plenty of snow today!

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,904
Well, you've come to New Zealand's premier snow and ski centre,

5
00:00:16,904 --> 00:00:21,400
and we've a whole load of activities for you during your week here.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,504
Most visitors come here for the cross-country skiing,

7
00:00:24,504 --> 00:00:28,180
where you're on fairly flat ground for most of the time

8
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:31,100
rather than going down steep mountainsides.

9
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:34,412
There are marked trails, but you can also leave these

10
00:00:34,412 --> 00:00:39,050
and go off on your own and that's an experience not to be missed.

11
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:41,280
You can go at your own speed

12
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:45,295
it's great aerobic exercise if you really push yourself,

13
00:00:45,295 --> 00:00:51,150
or if you prefer you can just glide gently along and enjoy the beautiful scenery.

14
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:55,150
This afternoon, you'll be going on a dog-sled trip.

15
00:00:55,150 --> 00:01:00,750
You may have seen our dogs on TV recently racing in the winter sled festival.

16
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:04,460
If you want, you can have your own team for the afternoon

17
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:06,690
and learn how to drive them,

18
00:01:06,690 --> 00:01:09,000
following behind our leader on the trail.

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,360
Or if you'd prefer, you can just sit back in the sled

20
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:14,700
and enjoy the ride as a passenger.

21
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:20,150
At the weekend, we have the team relay event, and you're all welcome to join in.

22
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:25,850
We have a local school coming along, and a lot of the teachers are taking part too.

23
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:29,500
Participation rather than winning is the main focus

24
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:32,650
and there's a medal for everyone who takes part.

25
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,740
Participants are in teams of two to four

26
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:39,500
and each team must complete four laps of the course.

27
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:44,883
For your final expedition, you'll head off to Mount Frenner wearing a pair of special snow shoes

28
00:01:44,883 --> 00:01:48,050
which allow you to walk on top of the snow.

29
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,680
This is an area where miners once searched for gold

30
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:55,200
though there are very few traces of their work left now.

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,220
When the snow melts in summer

32
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:00,750
the mountain slopes are carpeted in flowers and plants.

33
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:03,533
It's a long ascent, though not too steep,

34
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:08,750
and walkers generally take a couple of days to get to the summit and return.

35
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:12,600
You'll spend the night in our hut half-way up the mountain.

36
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:15,900
That's included in your package for the stay.

37
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:20,400
It's got cooking facilities, firewood and water for drinking.

38
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:26,050
For washing, we recommend you use melted snow, though, to conserve supplies.

39
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:31,700
We can take your luggage up on our snowmobile for you for just ten dollars a person.

40
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:33,680
The hut has cooking facilities

41
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:36,528
so you can make a hot meal in the evening and morning,

42
00:02:36,528 --> 00:02:38,800
but you need to take your own food.

43
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,650
The weather on Mount Frenner can be very stormy.

44
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:47,600
In that case, stay in the hut - generally the storms don't last long.

45
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,982
Don't stress about getting back here to the centre in time to catch the airport bus -

46
00:02:52,982 --> 00:02:55,000
they'll probably not be running anyway.

47
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,220
We do have an emergency locator beacon in the hut

48
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:01,536
but only use that if it's a real emergency,

49
00:03:01,536 --> 00:03:03,600
like if someone's ill or injured.

50
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:10,500
Now, let me tell you something about the different ski trails you can follow during your stay here.

51
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:14,900
Highland Trail's directly accessible from where we are now.

52
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,420
This trail's been designed to give first-timers an experience they'll enjoy

53
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:21,731
regardless of their age or skill,

54
00:03:21,731 --> 00:03:25,750
but it's also ideal for experts to practise their technique.

55
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:29,680
Then there's Pine Trail... if you're nervous about skiing

56
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:32,100
leave this one to the experts!

57
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:37,200
You follow a steep valley looking right down on the river below - scary!

58
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:42,050
But if you've fully mastered the techniques needed for hills, it's great fun.

59
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:47,050
Stony Trail's a good choice once you've got a general idea of the basics.

60
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:51,600
There are one or two tricky sections, but nothing too challenging.

61
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:54,520
There's a shelter half-way where you can sit

62
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:57,800
and take a break and enjoy the afternoon sunshine.

63
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,900
And finally, Loser's Trail.

64
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:03,719
This starts off following a gentle river valley

65
00:04:03,719 --> 00:04:08,652
but the last part is quite exposed so the snow conditions can be challenging -

66
00:04:08,652 --> 00:04:10,840
if it's snowing or windy

67
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:15,700
check with us before you set out to make sure the trail's open that day.

68
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:20,400
Right, so now if you'd like to follow me, we'll get started...
